  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/genidens_genidens.htm",
      "html": "<ul><li>Common name: Guri Sea Catfish</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1543",
      "html": "<p>Genidens genidens (Cuvier, 1829), the Guri Sea Catfish. Type locality: Tropical America. Maximum size 350 mm SL.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/genidens_genidens.htm",
      "html": "<p>Western Atlantic: Guyana south to La Plata River estuary (Argentina).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1543",
      "html": "<p>Southwest Atlantic: Atlantic draining rivers of southern South America, including Southern Atlantic Draining Rivers and Guanabara Bay.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/genidens_genidens.htm",
      "html": "<p>The Guri Sea Catfish (Genidens genidens) can be found off the coast of southern Bahia, Brazil, and Uruguay.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1543",
      "html": "<p>Brackish water.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1543",
      "html": "<p>Males have larger heads and mouths than females, probably due to their different roles during reproduction: males are mouth-brooders, incubating fertilized eggs and juveniles in their mouths.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/genidens_genidens.htm",
      "html": "<p>They migrate in large schools to fresh water, where they spawn in spring. The males carry out incubation of the eggs and leave their young in fresh water, where they grow and develop, to later return to the sea.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1543",
      "html": "<p>Unreported in the aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/genidens_genidens.htm",
      "html": "<p>The Guri Sea Catfish (Genidens genidens) is assessed as Least Concern based on its wide range along the eastern coast of South America. This species is harvested throughout most of its range as a food source. It is reported as abundant in areas of its range, but the species has a low fecundity which may make it susceptible to fishing pressure. Further research and monitoring of the harvest levels, fishing effort and population numbers is needed to confirm whether any serious population declines are occurring; if this proves to be the case then a reassessment will result in the species being moved into a higher threat category (IUCN 2009).</p><p>Etymology: The specific name genidens: génys, jaw; dens (L.), tooth, referring to the moveable teeth on palate.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/genidens_genidens.htm",
      "html": "<p>Acero, A. &amp; Bentancur, R. 2010. Genidens genidens (errata version published in 2017). The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2010.</p><p>Ferraris, C.J. Jr., 2007. Checklist of catfishes, recent and fossil (Osteichthyes: Siluriformes), and catalogue of siluriform primary types. Zootaxa 1418:1-628.</p><p>Froese, R. and D. Pauly. Editors. 2023. FishBase. World Wide Web electronic publication. www.fishbase.org, ( 10/2023 ).</p><p>Mazzoni, R., J. Petito and J.C. Miranda, 2000. Reproductive biology of Genidens genidens, a catfish from the Maricá Lagoon, RJ. Ciéncia e Cultura 52(2):121-126.</p>"
    },
